# L §5.16 (round 12): "mean(e) over the paths prints the mean with its standard error s/√N ... with N = 1 the
# standard error is none". emp is empirical over two equal values (2, 2), so every path draws 2 and the mean of one
# path is 2. (Was REFCORE-UNSPECIFIED §5.16, writer 5's T-79.)
# (Writer 8: empirical is gone (L §2.3); emp is now discrete(eq[k] for k in ks), eq = 1 at both keys, and its
# value is read as ev = vals[emp], the form L §5.16 gives for "one of n known values drawn with equal weight",
# e[discrete(1 for k in IDX)]: the same uniforms, and the samples 0 and 2 as before, emp itself now ka or kb.)
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"
unit PLN: currency minor 0.01

index ks = [ka, kb]
input w[k in ks]: num
input vals[k in ks]: num
input eq[k in ks]: num
input q25: num
input neg: num
input y1: num
def draw: dist = discrete(w[k] for k in ks) at reader
def emp: dist = discrete(eq[k] for k in ks) at reader
def ev: num = vals[emp] at reader

version v
  cite s
  governs 2026..
  known 2026-01-01
  w[ka] = 1
  w[kb] = 1
  vals[ka] = 2
  vals[kb] = 2
  eq[*] = 1
  q25 = 0.25
  neg = -1
  y1 = 1

ask a: mean(ev) law 2026-06-30 paths 1 seed 3
=== expect
a: 2 (standard error none; 1 paths; horizon 2026-06-30)
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2026-12-31
